Research complete. Here is the trace of bug 4127, with the root cause identified.

## 1. `CGR Rental Mgt` — Return procedure

`C:\workspace\Rental\src\RentalMgt.Codeunit.al` lines 43-58:

```al
43	    procedure Return(ContractNo: Code[20]; ReturnKm: Integer; DamageDescription: Text[100])
44	    var
45	        Contract: Record "CGR Rental Contract";
46	        CoreEvents: Codeunit "CGR Core Events";
47	    begin
48	        Contract.Get(ContractNo);
49	        if Contract.Status <> Contract.Status::"Checked Out" then
50	            Error(WrongStatusErr, ContractNo, Contract.Status::"Checked Out");
51	        if ReturnKm < Contract."Start Km" then
52	            Error(ReturnKmErr, Format(ReturnKm, 0, 9), Format(Contract."Start Km", 0, 9));
53	        Contract."Return Km" := ReturnKm;
54	        Contract."Damage Description" := DamageDescription;
55	        Contract.Status := Contract.Status::Returned;
56	        Contract.Modify(true);
57	        CoreEvents.RaiseVehicleReturned(Contract."Vehicle No.", ReturnKm, DamageDescription);
58	    end;
```

It doesn't touch the Vehicle record itself — it delegates to the `OnAfterVehicleReturned` event (`C:\workspace\Core\src\CoreEvents.Codeunit.al` lines 8-11, 18-21).

The actual vehicle-side handling is in `C:\workspace\Fleet\src\FleetReturnHandler.Codeunit.al` (codeunit 70103), which subscribes to that event — **this is where the bug is**:

```al
1	codeunit 70103 "CGR Fleet Return Handler"
2	{
3	    [EventSubscriber(ObjectType::Codeunit, Codeunit::"CGR Core Events", 'OnAfterVehicleReturned', '', false, false)]
4	    local procedure HandleVehicleReturned(VehicleNo: Code[20]; ReturnKm: Integer; DamageDescription: Text[100])
5	    var
6	        Vehicle: Record "CGR Vehicle";
7	        DamageMgt: Codeunit "CGR Damage Mgt";
8	    begin
9	        if not Vehicle.Get(VehicleNo) then
10	            exit;
11	        if DamageDescription <> '' then
12	            DamageMgt.RegisterDamage(VehicleNo, DamageDescription);
13	        Vehicle."Checked Out" := false;
14	        Vehicle.Mileage := ReturnKm;
15	        Vehicle.Modify(true);
16	    end;
17	}
```

**Root cause:** `Vehicle` is `Get()`'d at line 9, *before* `DamageMgt.RegisterDamage` runs. `RegisterDamage` (via the `OnAfterDamageRegistered` event chain, see below) blocks the vehicle and increments "Open Damages" — but it does so through its own separate `Record "CGR Vehicle"` instance inside the subscriber, and writes it with `Modify(true)`. Back in `HandleVehicleReturned`, the original stale `Vehicle` buffer (still holding `Blocked = false`, `"Open Damages" = 0` from before the damage was registered) is then modified for `"Checked Out"`/`Mileage` and saved with `Vehicle.Modify(true)` at line 15 — which overwrites the blocking/damage-count changes just made by the subscriber, since AL record variables don't auto-refresh from other writers. Net effect: damage entry is created, but Blocked and "Open Damages" get reset to their pre-damage values.

