`RepairDamage` is the "intended pattern" for recomputing `"Open Damages"` (recounts open `CGR Damage Entry` rows for the vehicle) and `Blocked` (true if any open damages remain) — this is the reusable logic that should govern the counter/flag whenever damage state changes.

## 4. Reusable "block vehicle" logic that isn't being applied correctly on return

`C:\workspace\Fleet\src\VehicleBlockSubscriber.Codeunit.al` (codeunit 70104):

```al
1	codeunit 70104 "CGR Vehicle Block Subscriber"
2	{
3	    [EventSubscriber(ObjectType::Codeunit, Codeunit::"CGR Damage Mgt", 'OnAfterDamageRegistered', '', false, false)]
4	    local procedure BlockDamagedVehicle(var DamageEntry: Record "CGR Damage Entry")
5	    var
6	        Vehicle: Record "CGR Vehicle";
7	    begin
8	        if not Vehicle.Get(DamageEntry."Vehicle No.") then
9	            exit;
10	        Vehicle."Open Damages" += 1;
11	        Vehicle.Blocked := true;
12	        Vehicle.Modify(true);
13	    end;
14	}
```

This subscriber *does* correctly increment `"Open Damages"` and set `Blocked := true` whenever `CGR Damage Mgt.RegisterDamage` inserts a new damage entry — and `RegisterDamage` is in fact called from `FleetReturnHandler.HandleVehicleReturned` (line 12 above). So the block/count logic does fire during a damaged return. The bug is purely the ordering/stale-record issue in `FleetReturnHandler.Codeunit.al`: the outer `Vehicle` record is `Get()`'d before `RegisterDamage` runs and then blindly `Modify(true)`'d afterward with its stale (pre-damage) `Blocked`/`"Open Damages"` values, clobbering what `VehicleBlockSubscriber` just wrote to the database.

**Fix direction (not applied, per instructions):** in `C:\workspace\Fleet\src\FleetReturnHandler.Codeunit.al`, either re-`Get()` (or `Find()`) the `Vehicle` record after calling `DamageMgt.RegisterDamage` (before setting `"Checked Out"`/`Mileage` and calling `Modify`), or reorder so the mileage/checked-out update and `Modify` happen before `RegisterDamage` is invoked, so the subscriber's write isn't overwritten. Test coverage exists for the no-damage case at `C:\workspace\Test\src\RentalTests.Codeunit.al` lines 44-59 (`ReturnWithoutDamageReleasesVehicle`), but there is no equivalent test asserting `Blocked`/`"Open Damages"` for the damaged-return case — worth adding once fixed.

